# WHAT EVERY DEPLOYED BOX OF THIS INSTANCE DECLARES, whichever environment it is.
#
# `bin/deploy.sh <env>` assembles the box's `.env` from THREE halves, in this order:
#
#   1 · this file              — true of every deployed box (bench-only switches turned off, paths, ports)
#   2 · deploy/<env>.env       — what differs between the two boxes: the host, the six hostnames, the bucket
#   3 · what the BOX already has — the keys a BIRTH wrote there (store ids, host maps, sibling lists). They
#                                  are DERIVED, they are knowable only after the box has been seeded, and a
#                                  deploy that overwrote them would take the shop down for a reason with no
#                                  name on it. `bin/deploy.sh` carries them across; see THE DERIVED HALF there.
#
# ⛔ NOTHING SECRET GOES IN EITHER FILE. Secrets reach the containers through `env-source.sh`, which is sourced
# ON THE BOX and reads them from `.secrets` beside it — a file `bin/deploy.sh` never sends and never reads.
#
# ★ THIS IS NOT A SECOND COMPOSE. Measured 2026-09-16 on this repository: `compose.yml` already IS the
# deployment stack and every bench-ism in it is a variable, not a line — `FORGE_CADDYFILE` chooses the edge
# (the PRODUCTION Caddyfile is the default, `compose.yml:426`), `FORGE_BENCH_BIND` chooses the interface
# (`compose.yml:370`), and the mail collector is behind `profiles: ['bench-mailbox']` so a deployment never
# creates it (`compose.yml:509`). A deployment therefore runs THE SAME two files the bench runs, with this
# `.env` instead of that one. A second compose file would be a second truth about what this box is made of,
# and the day they disagreed the bench would still be green.

# ── the edge ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The PRODUCTION Caddyfile: named rather than defaulted. It is already compose's default, and a deployment
# that relied on the default would be a deployment whose edge could be changed by editing a default.
FORGE_CADDYFILE=./caddy/Caddyfile

# ⚠️ EMPTY MEANS EVERY INTERFACE, AND FOR A PUBLIC BOX THAT IS THE ONLY LEGAL ANSWER. The bench says
# `127.0.0.1` because every door it publishes is plain HTTP and a browser silently drops a `Secure` cookie on
# such an origin; a deployed box terminates TLS at the edge, so the trap that variable exists for is gone.
#
# ⚠️ AND IT PUBLISHES MORE THAN :80 AND :443 — READ THIS BEFORE ASSUMING THE BOX IS CLOSED. One variable
# carries all five published ports of `compose.yml` plus the totem's in `compose.override.yml`, deliberately
# (compose.yml:370 carries the argument: "this box publishes on <this interface>" is ONE decision). So a
# deployed box also publishes the three BENCH doors — 8081→:81, 8202→:83, 8203→:82 — on every interface.
# Nothing answers on them (`caddy/Caddyfile` declares no `:81`, `:82` or `:83` site, so the edge refuses the
# connection), and MEASURED on the staging box 2026-09-16 they are not reachable from the internet at all:
# `:80` and `:443` connect from off the box and `:8081`, `:8202` and `:8203` do not, while all five are open
# on `127.0.0.1` from inside it. ⚠️ SOMETHING IN FRONT IS CLOSING THEM AND THIS REPOSITORY DOES NOT DECLARE
# IT — `ufw` on that host allows only 22, which Docker's own DNAT normally walks around, so the block is
# either the provider's firewall group or the host's nftables and neither is in `bin/provision-host.sh`. A
# closed door nobody wrote down is a door that opens when somebody rebuilds the host. Recorded, not relied
# on: see RESULTADOS-d1.md.
FORGE_BENCH_BIND=
FORGE_HTTP_PORT=80
FORGE_HTTPS_PORT=443

# ── which tenant the admin serves ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EMPTY = host mode: this box carries TWO brands and one admin container, so the request's Host decides which
# admin is being asked for. It needs `FORGE_ADMIN_PLATFORM_TOKEN` (env-source.sh) and one admin-directory row
# per brand, both of which the birth creates. `?` without a colon is what makes empty a DECLARATION and an
# absent variable an accident — see compose.yml's own note on the line that reads it.
FORGE_ADMIN_TENANT=

# ── the bootstrap tenant and its first store (read ONLY by `dist/provision-ref.js`) ─────────────────────────
# The one-shot that creates the tenant, the first store and the first operator before any credential exists.
# Every other store of this box is created afterwards, through the port, from `seed/box.json`.
FORGE_REF_TENANT=forgeco
FORGE_REF_STORE_HANDLE=forge
FORGE_REF_STORE_NAME=Forge
# ⚠️ DELIBERATELY EMPTY IN THE REPOSITORY. It is a real person's inbox — the first operator's — and this
# repository is source-available. It is written into the box's own `.env` by whoever runs the bootstrap, and
# `bin/deploy.sh` carries it across from there like every other derived value.
FORGE_ADMIN_SEED_EMAIL=
FORGE_ADMIN_SEED_NAME=

# ── what the containers mount ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FORGE_EXTENSIONS_DIR=/app/extensions
FORGE_EXTENSIONS_HOST_DIR=./extensions
# ── ★★ THE EXAMPLE CATALOGUE, AND THE THREE LINES ARE A PAIR OF FILESYSTEMS ────────────────────────────────
#
# `_HOST_DIR` is where the box keeps it, `_DIR` is where the kernel sees it mounted, and the third is where the
# 3.6 GB of photographs the seed hydrates lands (a named volume, off the read-only dataset mount).
#
# ⛔ THE DATASET IS THE BIRTH'S CARGO, NOT THE DEPLOY'S — `bin/birth-remote.sh` step 0c delivers it, and
# `bin/deploy.sh`'s DELIVER list deliberately does not. A pin bump has no business moving a catalogue, and a
# deploy that carried 40 MB of JSON every time would make the cheap gesture the expensive one.
#
# ⚠️ AND `_DIR` EMPTY IS NOT A NEUTRAL DEFAULT: `compose.yml:119` forwards it as `${FORGE_SEED_DATASET_DIR:-}`
# and `dist/seed-demo.js` reads an unset one as «nothing to seed», exits 0, and the box is born with no
# catalogue at all — green. That is why it is declared here rather than left to the compose default.
FORGE_SEED_DATASET_HOST_DIR=./seed/dataset
FORGE_SEED_DATASET_DIR=/app/seed-dataset
FORGE_SEED_PHOTOS_DIR=/data/seed-photos

# ── ⛔⛔ THE MASSIVE SEED'S ACTION CEILING, AND IT IS HERE BECAUSE A NUMBER OF *SIZE* DOES NOT TRAVEL ───────
#
# `bin/birth-remote.sh` step 9 (and `bin/box-up.sh` step 9) raise `FORGE_EXTENSION_ACTION_TIMEOUT_MS` for the
# one-shot that fills the catalogue. The default they carry — 1 800 000 ms, 30 min — was DERIVED ON A LAPTOP:
# «1 287 products written in 300 s, so 2 790 need ~650 s; 1 800 000 is nearly 3× that».
#
# ⛔ MEASURED ON THE STAGING BOX 2026-09-16, AND THE NUMBER DID NOT TRAVEL. One vCPU wrote **2 225 of 2 790**
# products in the thirty minutes that ceiling allows — ~74/min against the laptop's ~257/min, 3,5× slower —
# and the birth came out of step 9 with a HALF-WRITTEN CATALOGUE. That failure is documented at the step and
# it has no one-line repair: the timeout does not CANCEL the action (`timeout.ts`: the caller stops waiting,
# the writer does not stop), and the entrypoint's `finally` then closes the pool underneath an abandoned
# writer.
#
# ⇒ DERIVED FROM THIS BOX, NOT GUESSED: 2 790 at the measured rate is ~38 min, plus ~4 min of hydration and
# media. 7 200 000 ms (2 h) is ~3× that, the same head-room the laptop's number had.
#
# ⚠️ AND IT IS STILL A NUMBER OF SIZE, WHICH IS THE SHAPE THIS HOUSE HAS FORBIDDEN SINCE 2026-09-11. The
# durable answer is not a bigger number here: it is for the entrypoint to be judged by PROGRESS — a window of
# NON-progress — the way `pk35/p1` demoted the warmer's `max_duration_ms` to an optional net. That lives in
# the PRODUCT. This line is a bridge, it is named as one, and it is in a file rather than in somebody's
# memory because every deployed box of this instance meets the same wall.
FORGE_SEED_ACTION_TIMEOUT_MS=7200000
FORGE_THEMES_DIR=/app/themes-instance
FORGE_THEMES_HOST_DIR=./themes
FORGE_ADMIN_I18N_HOST_DIR=./i18n
FORGE_TLS_CERT_DIR=./caddy/certs
FORGE_CADDY_EXTRA_DIR=./caddy/extra

# ── the platform face stays shut ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Empty is "nobody": the Caddyfile's own default is loopback, so `/v1/control/*` answers 403 from the public
# interface. A VPN or bastion CIDR goes here and nowhere else.
FORGE_CONTROL_ALLOW_CIDR=

# ── the break-glass admin door ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⛔ OFF. On the bench it is `1` so an operator can paste a raw token at /login/ops. A box a browser on the
# internet can reach does not get that door: it is a login screen whose only factor is a bearer token.
FORGE_ADMIN_OPS_LOGIN=

# ── rate limits ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Empty = the kernel's own defaults, which are the numbers a production box should keep. The bench raises the
# anonymous-write ceiling to 100 000 because SEEDING writes thousands of rows through that exact door; a
# deployed box that is seeded from a laptop hits the same ceiling, and THAT is a knob the birth should be
# raising for the length of the seed rather than a number this file leaves permanently open.
FORGE_RATE_LIMIT_PER_CREDENTIAL=
FORGE_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=
FORGE_PUBLIC_WRITE_LIMIT_PER_IP=
FORGE_PUBLIC_WRITE_WINDOW_SECONDS=

# ── what only a BIRTH can know, declared as the sentinel the birth already understands ─────────────────────
#
# ⛔⛔ EMPTY WOULD KILL THIS BOX BEFORE THE FIRST CONTAINER, and `sto_PENDING_SEED` is not a placeholder this
# file invented. `compose.override.yml:140` DEMANDS the counter's store id, compose interpolates the whole
# file on every command, and a variable that is present and blank is refused exactly as an absent one is
# (measured 2026-09-05, `.env.example:349`). The sentinel is what `bin/box-up.sh:2024` reads as «the counter
# has no store yet, so do not start the totem and SAY SO» — `bin/deploy.sh` reads it the same way, because a
# second vocabulary for "not provisioned yet" is two things to keep in step.
#
# ⇒ A DEPLOY NEVER REPLACES A REAL ID WITH THIS. `bin/deploy.sh` carries the box's own `.env` across, and a
# declared key wins over a carried one — so this line is written on a box the birth has not reached and the
# birth's line survives on a box it has. That is the only asymmetry in the whole assembly and it is here.
FORGE_TOTEM_STORE_ID=sto_PENDING_SEED
FORGE_TOTEM_STORE_HANDLE=balcao
# ⚠️ SOFT, UNLIKE THE ONE ABOVE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS DELIBERATE (compose.override.yml:65). A totem with no
# store is the wrong shop and must not boot; a vitrine with no overlay is the vitrine as it was before the
# café had its own institutional pages — every page still answers 200, in the body shared with the reference
# storefront. The birth writes the real id here.
FORGE_COFFEE_STORE_ID=

# ── media ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⛔⛔ THE `s3` DRIVER IS NOT IN THE KERNEL THIS BOX PINS, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT OF 2026-09-16, not a
# preference. `forge.lock` pins images built from the product at `9c71bb4cb`, and
# `apps/api/src/storage-connector.ts` AT THAT COMMIT knows exactly two drivers, `gcs` and `local` — the `s3`
# driver (phase F1 of the INFRA-EXEMPLAR spec) landed in the product afterwards. Setting FORGE_STORAGE_DRIVER
# to `s3` against this pin gives a kernel that refuses the name at boot, so the bucket below is DECLARED and
# the driver is left unset: media falls back to the local driver, exactly as the bench's does.
#
# ⇒ R2 becomes real the day this instance re-bakes its four images from a product that carries the driver.
# That is one gesture (`bash bin/build-local.sh <forge checkout>`) and no change to this file beyond the two
# lines below, plus the two key names in `env-source.sh` — which are NOT there yet, on purpose: a secret
# nothing can read is a name that looks configured. See RESULTADOS-d1.md.
FORGE_STORAGE_DRIVER=
FORGE_STORAGE_REGION=auto
FORGE_MEDIA_BASE_URL=
